{
  "_doc": "Studio service bench, OCR slot: POST /api/services/ocr (multipart) must recognise text in an uploaded page and return a persisted run. Added 2026-11-02: the slot worked in the browser but had no automated proof, and this is the only route family the old probe harness could not even send (it hardcoded a JSON body). The fixture is a generated 720x160 PNG reading 'SERVICE LAB OCR 2026' \u2014 inline base64 so the probe needs no side file in the job image or the ConfigMap.",
  "service_probe": {
    "service": "orchestrator",
    "method": "POST",
    "path": "/api/services/ocr",
    "auth": "license",
    "timeout_seconds": 180,
    "multipart": {
      "fields": {},
      "files": [
        {
          "field": "file",
          "filename": "service-lab-ocr.png",
          "content_type": "image/png",
          "content_b64": ""
        }
      ]
    },
    "expect_status_code": 200,
    "expect_keys": [
      "id",
      "slot",
      "status",
      "created_at",
      "text"
    ],
    "checks": [
      {
        "type": "equals",
        "path": "slot",
        "value": "ocr"
      },
      {
        "type": "equals",
        "path": "status",
        "value": "succeeded"
      },
      {
        "type": "equals",
        "path": "output_kind",
        "value": "markdown"
      },
      {
        "type": "nonempty",
        "path": "text"
      }
    ]
  },
  "_assert_doc": "`output_kind` for OCR is `markdown` (service_lab/invoker.py::_ocr), which the frontend renders as plain text \u2014 that is a rendering choice, not a defect, and it is asserted here so a silent change of shape is visible. `text` must be NON-EMPTY: the fixture is unambiguous black text on white, so an empty transcript means the OCR path did not actually run, not that the sample was hard. If a future provider returns nothing for a clean synthetic page, that is a finding to report rather than an assertion to relax.",
  "_cost_doc": "Local MLX OCR (mlx-api-media), no paid API. `nightly: false` + `rotation: false`: proof of a shipped feature, not a per-night regression."
}
